wers we  will do how concerned are you that  people are going to  freeze from the cold this winter at home  because they can't afford to afford to  put their energy on i'm deeply concerned  i mean those people on pre-pay meters  i've we've got to make sure we that's  why i go back and say to you my my  preference is targeting the help um you  know labor have talked about sort of  universal uh uh piece of help that would  cost about  but when i ask you it you won't tell me  so when will we know no what i'm saying  to you is actually it's better to target  the help for those families that really  need it as we're doing now with the 8  million families getting 1200 pounds um  target it for smes so hospitality high  energy use sectors maybe some larger  businesses who are also  finding it very difficult when your  energy bill has gone up by three and a  half times four times it's very  difficult to be able to sustain your  business and i don't want to see that  scarring effect that we could be left  with  in the economy so all that work we will  make sure is ready for the incoming  prime minister um that's the right thing  to do and then of course in the longer  term we have an energy strategy that  ensures that we have that involves  nuclear resilience very much involves  nuclear it involves offshore wind it  involves of course tidal energy i went  to visit a company in scotland that has  actually commercialized a tidal energy  the beauty of tidal energy is actually  you can use it for base load because you  can predict the tide unlike wind and sun  uh tidal energy is is is reasonably  predictable so  lots of investment in uh offshore wind  we've seen an increase in our gas  production by about 26 percent this year  because of the policies that  quasi-cuatang uh put in place  um  by the way well that's up to the  incoming prime minister if liz trusts  the prime minister then she'll have to  decide her cabinet do you want to remain  chancellor i will serve wherever uh l